"""Built-in demo notes so judges can try the agent in one message ("sample")."""

SAMPLE = {
    "title": "Photosynthesis",
    "notes": """Photosynthesis is the process plants, algae and some bacteria use to turn light energy into chemical energy stored in sugar. Overall: 6 CO2 + 6 H2O + light -> C6H12O6 + 6 O2. It happens in chloroplasts.

Light-dependent reactions happen in the thylakoid membranes. Chlorophyll absorbs light (mostly red and blue; green is reflected, which is why leaves look green). The energy splits water molecules (photolysis), releasing oxygen as a by-product, electrons and H+ ions. Electrons move along an electron transport chain, which pumps H+ into the thylakoid space; ATP synthase uses the H+ gradient to make ATP. NADP+ picks up electrons to become NADPH.

The Calvin cycle (light-independent reactions) happens in the stroma. The enzyme RuBisCO fixes CO2 onto RuBP. Using ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, the fixed carbon is reduced to G3P, some of which leaves the cycle to build glucose; the rest regenerates RuBP so the cycle can continue.

Connections: the light reactions supply the ATP and NADPH the Calvin cycle needs, and the Calvin cycle returns ADP and NADP+ to the light reactions. Without light, the Calvin cycle stops soon after because it runs out of ATP and NADPH. The oxygen we breathe comes from the split water, not from CO2.

Limiting factors: light intensity, CO2 concentration and temperature (enzymes like RuBisCO work best in a range).""",
}
